WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.229 --> 00:00:05.030
Now that we know a little bit about what the Merge Actor tool does,

2
00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:09.230
I'd like to talk about the Hierarchical Level of Detail tool, or HLOD.

3
00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:17.760
HLOD is a way to replace lots of individual static mesh actors with one single static mesh actor that has one material,

4
00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:21.199
and because of this, it's going to be cheaper to render.

5
00:00:21.199 --> 00:00:25.589
This tool works by going through all of the static meshes placed in your level,

6
00:00:25.589 --> 00:00:29.420
and it will group them based on their distance from one another.

7
00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:36.420
It will then create new mesh and materials to replace the static meshes in those groups,

8
00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:43.109
so that as the user progresses through the world, distance detail will be cheaper to render,

9
00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:52.320
and as the user gets closer and closer to that detail, it will then be replaced with the original static meshes.

10
00:00:52.320 --> 00:01:02.320
Again, the main reason to use these tools are to help lessen the rendering cost of your level's objects and distance.

11
00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:12.739
Now to access the HLOD tools, or the Hierarchical Level of Detail Outliner,

12
00:01:12.739 --> 00:01:18.739
you need to go to Window, and then select Hierarchical LOD Outliner from the menu.

13
00:01:18.739 --> 00:01:23.739
If this option is grayed out, that means you are using a level that works with World Partition,

14
00:01:23.739 --> 00:01:31.060
and we will cover how to set up HLOD with World Partition later on in this video tutorial.

15
00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:35.060
Let's go ahead and pop over to the editor now and have a look at how this works.

16
00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:41.060
Now inside of the Unreal Engine 5 editor, let's look at how we can utilize HLOD.

